{%- comment -%}
  fye-stone-product — the product page for a LOOSE STONE.

  Built 01/09/2026. Serves BOTH loose diamonds (~14,200) and loose gemstones
  (~13,200): verified that both product types carry templateSuffix "diamond",
  so one template and one section cover about 27,000 pages.

  Named fye-STONE-product, not fye-diamond-product as live has it, because
  half the pages it renders are sapphires, rubies and emeralds.

  ── WHY THIS IS NOT A BRANCH OF main-product ──────────────────────────────

  Every other product on this store is a ring. A loose stone shares almost
  nothing with one:

      rings                        a loose stone
      metal / carat / quality      ONE variant, one price
      ring size                    none
      engraving at £55             none
      oversize surcharge           none
      centre / side choosers       none

  Ed, 01/09/2026: no engraving and no ring size on this page. So there is no
  <form> full of options — the buy box is a price, a button, and the facts.
  The page's substance is the CHARACTERISTICS TABLE and the certificate.

  ── THE TABLE IS AN ALLOW-LIST. NEVER LOOP THE NAMESPACE. ─────────────────

  **This is the most important line in the file.** The `fye` namespace holds
  commercial data next to the display fields. Measured 01/09/2026 on
  old-miner-fancy-yellow-natural-diamond-1-29ct-si2:

      fye.trade_cost       999.54     <- what we paid
      fye.price_multiple   1.62       <- our margin
      fye.nivoda_id        8186B36A3  <- supplier reference

  A table built with `for f in product.metafields.fye` would publish our cost
  price and markup on 27,000 public pages. Every row below is named
  explicitly, and a new metafield appears on the page only when someone adds
  it here on purpose. If you are tempted to "simplify" this into a loop: that
  is the bug.

  ── WHAT DISTINGUISHES A STONE ────────────────────────────────────────────

  `fye.stone_family` is the discriminator, and it is real data rather than a
  guess from product.type (which has caused this class of bug here before):

      "Gemstone"              sapphire, ruby, emerald…
      "Fancy Colour Diamond"  graded by hue, not by the D–J scale
      absent                  a white diamond

  Gemstones use the SAME clarity field with a different vocabulary — "Eye
  Clean", "Slightly Included" rather than VS1 — so one row serves both. What
  differs is which rows exist at all, and every row is render-if-present, so
  a sapphire simply has no fluorescence line rather than an empty one.

  ── AVAILABILITY ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Every stone sampled reports inventory 0 with policy DENY. Whether that makes
  `available` false depends on whether inventory is TRACKED, which the admin
  API did not expose in the same query — and the ring picker, which filters on
  availability, demonstrably shows stones. So the page follows main-product's
  pattern: add to bag when available, the enquiry label when not. Correct
  either way, and worth confirming before launch.

  ── UNANSWERED, AND WHY THE PAGE STILL WORKS ──────────────────────────────

  Ed has not yet said what "set this in a ring" should do, or whether a stone
  can be bought on its own. Both are section SETTINGS with no default link, so
  the secondary button does not render until one is supplied — rather than
  shipping a guess as a live button on 27,000 pages.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign s = section.settings
  assign p = product
  assign mf = p.metafields.fye
  assign v = p.selected_or_first_available_variant

  assign family = mf.stone_family
  assign is_gem = false
  assign is_fancy = false
  if family == 'Gemstone'
    assign is_gem = true
  elsif family == 'Fancy Colour Diamond'
    assign is_fancy = true
  endif

  comment
    The fancy grading phrase, in trade order: intensity, overtone, hue —
    "Fancy Intense Brownish Yellow". Built from whatever exists; a stone with
    only a hue reads simply "Yellow".
  endcomment
  capture fancy_phrase
    if mf.fancy_intensity != blank
      echo mf.fancy_intensity
      echo ' '
    endif
    if mf.fancy_overtone != blank
      echo mf.fancy_overtone
      echo ' '
    endif
    echo mf.fancy_hue
  endcapture
  assign fancy_phrase = fancy_phrase | strip

  comment
    Carat is formatted by the same snippet the ring pages use, so 0.50 reads
    "0.5" here exactly as it does in the picker. It also renders NOTHING for
    zero, which in Liquid is not blank — see fye-carat-label.
  endcomment
  capture ct
    render 'fye-carat-label', value: mf.carat.value
  endcapture
  assign ct = ct | strip

  comment
    Breadcrumb collection: the route actually taken, else the first real
    collection. Same rule as main-product.
  endcomment
  assign crumb_col = collection
  unless crumb_col
    for c in p.collections
      unless c.handle == 'all'
        assign crumb_col = c
        break
      endunless
    endfor
  endunless

  comment
    The one-line identity, in the SAME word order the diamond picker uses for
    its cards (stoneTitle in fye-ui.js: shape, carat, colour, clarity). A
    shopper meets a stone as a card and then as a page; if the two disagree
    the stone appears to change.
  endcomment
  capture identity
    echo mf.shape
    if ct != blank
      echo ' · '
      echo ct
      echo 'ct'
    endif
    if is_fancy and fancy_phrase != blank
      echo ' · '
      echo fancy_phrase
    elsif mf.colour != blank
      echo ' · '
      echo mf.colour
    endif
    if mf.clarity != blank
      echo ' · '
      echo mf.clarity
    endif
  endcapture

  comment
    What to call this stone in a sentence. The enquiry button reads "Contact us
    about this diamond" on a diamond and "…this sapphire" on a sapphire —
    calling a ruby a diamond in a button is the kind of error customers notice.
  endcomment
  assign stone_noun = 'diamond'
  if is_gem
    assign stone_noun = 'gemstone'
    if mf.gem_type != blank
      assign stone_noun = mf.gem_type | downcase
    endif
  endif

  assign spin = mf.video_url
-%}

<section class="band band--white stone" data-screen-label="Loose stone">
  <div class="wrap">

    <nav class="stone__crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="{{ routes.root_url }}">Home</a></li>
        {%- if crumb_col -%}
          <li><a href="{{ crumb_col.url }}">{{ crumb_col.title }}</a></li>
        {%- endif -%}
        <li><span aria-current="page">{{ p.title }}</span></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>
    {%- comment -%}
      The same trail as JSON-LD (audit 02/09/2026, D3), rendered beside the
      visible one so the two cannot drift.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- render 'schema-breadcrumbs', mid_name: crumb_col.title, mid_url: crumb_col.url, last_name: p.title, last_url: canonical_url -%}

    <div class="stone__top">

      {%- comment -%} ---- Media ---- {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="stone__gallery" data-fye-gallery>
        <div class="stone__stage">
          {%- if p.media.size > 0 -%}
            {%- for media in p.media limit: 6 -%}
              {%- comment -%}
                Loading and alt computed here, not chained inside the image_tag
                arguments: a filter after a named argument applies to the tag's
                OUTPUT (conventions §5), so the old chain emitted loading="true"
                / loading="false", which browsers read as eager, and every
                panel downloaded at once. Same fix as main-product (S1).
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- liquid
                if forloop.first
                  assign img_loading = 'eager'
                  assign img_priority = 'high'
                else
                  assign img_loading = 'lazy'
                  assign img_priority = 'auto'
                endif
                assign img_alt = media.alt | default: p.title
              -%}
              <div class="stone__panel{% if forloop.first %} is-on{% endif %}" data-fye-panel="{{ forloop.index0 }}">
                {{ media | image_url: width: 1200 | image_tag:
                   loading: img_loading,
                   fetchpriority: img_priority,
                   widths: '400,600,900,1200',
                   sizes: '(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 50vw',
                   alt: img_alt,
                   class: 'stone__img' }}
              </div>
            {%- endfor -%}
          {%- else -%}
            <div class="stone__panel is-on stone__panel--empty"></div>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            The supplier's 360 viewer. An IFRAME rather than a script embed:
            it is a third-party page (loupe360.com) and giving it its own
            document keeps its CSS and JS out of ours.

            `loading="lazy"` matters here — this panel is hidden until asked
            for, and 27,000 pages should not each open a connection to a
            viewer nobody has clicked.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if spin != blank -%}
            <div class="stone__panel stone__panel--spin" data-fye-panel="spin">
              <iframe class="stone__spin"
                      src="{{ spin }}"
                      title="360° view of {{ p.title | escape }}"
                      loading="lazy"
                      frameborder="0"
                      allowfullscreen></iframe>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>

        {%- if p.media.size > 1 or spin != blank -%}
          <ul class="stone__thumbs">
            {%- for media in p.media limit: 6 -%}
              <li>
                <button class="stone__thumb{% if forloop.first %} is-on{% endif %}"
                        type="button"
                        data-fye-thumb="{{ forloop.index0 }}"
                        aria-label="View image {{ forloop.index }}">
                  {{ media | image_url: width: 200 | image_tag:
                     loading: 'lazy', widths: '100,200', sizes: '88px', alt: '' }}
                </button>
              </li>
            {%- endfor -%}

            {%- if spin != blank -%}
              <li>
                <button class="stone__thumb stone__thumb--spin"
                        type="button"
                        data-fye-thumb="spin"
                        aria-label="View in 360 degrees">
                  {%- if p.featured_image != blank -%}
                    {{ p.featured_image | image_url: width: 200 | image_tag:
                       loading: 'lazy', widths: '100,200', sizes: '88px', alt: '' }}
                  {%- endif -%}
                  <span class="stone__spinbadge" aria-hidden="true">360°</span>
                </button>
              </li>
            {%- endif -%}
          </ul>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>

      {%- comment -%} ---- Buy box ---- {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="stone__buy">
        {%- if mf.origin != blank -%}
          <p class="stone__eyebrow">
            {{ mf.origin }}
            {%- if is_gem and mf.gem_type != blank %} {{ mf.gem_type }}{% elsif is_fancy %} fancy colour diamond{% elsif is_gem == false %} diamond{% endif -%}
          </p>
        {%- endif -%}

        <h1 class="stone__title">{{ p.title }}</h1>

        {%- if identity != blank -%}
          <p class="stone__identity">{{ identity }}</p>
        {%- endif -%}

        <p class="stone__price">{{ v.price | money }}</p>

        {%- comment -%}
          ---- The characteristics table ----
          EXPLICIT ROWS ONLY. See the header: fye.trade_cost and
          fye.price_multiple live in this namespace. Never loop it.

          A <table> and not a definition list, per Ed: this is tabular data
          with a consistent shape across stones, and it should read as a
          specification. Row headers are <th scope="row"> so a screen reader
          announces the field name with every value.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <table class="stone__spec">
          <caption class="stone__speccap">{{ s.spec_heading | default: 'Stone characteristics' }}</caption>
          <tbody>
            {%- if mf.shape != blank -%}
              <tr><th scope="row">Shape</th><td>{{ mf.shape }}</td></tr>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- if ct != blank -%}
              <tr><th scope="row">Carat</th><td>{{ ct }}ct</td></tr>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- comment -%}
              A fancy coloured diamond is graded by hue and intensity, so the
              D–J letter is meaningless on it — the ring picker drops the same
              field for the same reason. One row or the other, never both.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if is_fancy -%}
              {%- if fancy_phrase != blank -%}
                <tr><th scope="row">Colour</th><td>{{ fancy_phrase }}</td></tr>
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- elsif mf.colour != blank -%}
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Colour</th>
                <td>
                  {{ mf.colour }}
                  {%- if mf.colour_shade != blank %} · {{ mf.colour_shade }}{% endif -%}
                </td>
              </tr>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- if mf.clarity != blank -%}
              <tr><th scope="row">Clarity</th><td>{{ mf.clarity }}</td></tr>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- if mf.cut != blank -%}
              <tr><th scope="row">Cut</th><td>{{ mf.cut }}</td></tr>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- if mf.polish != blank -%}
              <tr><th scope="row">Polish</th><td>{{ mf.polish }}</td></tr>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- if mf.symmetry != blank -%}
              <tr><th scope="row">Symmetry</th><td>{{ mf.symmetry }}</td></tr>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- if mf.fluorescence != blank -%}
              <tr><th scope="row">Fluorescence</th><td>{{ mf.fluorescence }}</td></tr>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- comment -%} Gemstone-only rows. Absent on a diamond, so absent from its table. {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if mf.gem_type != blank -%}
              <tr><th scope="row">Gemstone</th><td>{{ mf.gem_type }}</td></tr>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- if mf.treatment != blank -%}
              <tr><th scope="row">Treatment</th><td>{{ mf.treatment }}</td></tr>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- if mf.mine_origin != blank -%}
              <tr><th scope="row">Mine origin</th><td>{{ mf.mine_origin }}</td></tr>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- if mf.origin != blank -%}
              <tr><th scope="row">Origin</th><td>{{ mf.origin }}</td></tr>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- comment -%}
              Certificate. "OTHER" appears as a lab name on some gemstones and
              tells a customer nothing, so it is shown as "Independent" rather
              than printed raw.

              cert_url exists on some stones and not others — where it does,
              the number becomes a link to the issuing lab's verification page,
              which is the whole point of a certificate.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              THE SERIAL IS NOT SHOWN — Ed, 01/09/2026. The lab is the
              reassurance; the serial is a reference the customer has no use for
              before buying, and on ~27,000 public pages it would publish a
              machine-readable index of our entire inventory.

              Where cert_url exists the lab name itself becomes the link, so
              verification is still one click and the serial is on the page it
              leads to.

              "OTHER" appears as a lab name on some gemstones and tells a
              customer nothing, so it reads "Independent" rather than raw.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if mf.cert_lab != blank -%}
              {%- capture lab_name -%}
                {% if mf.cert_lab == 'OTHER' %}Independent{% else %}{{ mf.cert_lab }}{% endif %}
              {%- endcapture -%}
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Certificate</th>
                <td>
                  {%- if mf.cert_url != blank -%}
                    <a href="{{ mf.cert_url }}" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">{{ lab_name | strip }} — verify</a>
                  {%- else -%}
                    {{ lab_name | strip }}
                  {%- endif -%}
                </td>
              </tr>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- if v.sku != blank -%}
              <tr><th scope="row">Reference</th><td>{{ v.sku }}</td></tr>
            {%- endif -%}
          </tbody>
        </table>

        {%- comment -%}
          ---- Buying, BELOW the table — Ed, 01/09/2026 ----
          A loose stone is bought on its specification, so the facts are read
          before the commitment. On a ring the options ARE the decision and the
          button follows them; here the table plays that part.

          One variant, no options, nothing to configure — so this form posts a
          fixed id and needs no JavaScript at all. No engraving, no size.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="stone__actions">
          {%- form 'product', p, class: 'stone__form' -%}
            <input type="hidden" name="id" value="{{ v.id }}">
            <button class="btn btn--block" type="submit" name="add"{% unless v.available %} disabled{% endunless %}>
              {%- if v.available -%}
                {{ s.atc_label | default: 'Add to bag' }}
              {%- else -%}
                {{ s.mto_label | default: 'Reserved — ask about similar stones' }}
              {%- endif -%}
            </button>
          {%- endform -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            The enquiry button. Unlike "set this in a ring", whose destination
            is still an open question, an enquiry has an obvious home — so this
            renders with a default rather than waiting on a setting.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- capture enquire_text -%}
            {%- if s.enquire_label != blank -%}
              {{ s.enquire_label }}
            {%- else -%}
              Contact us about this {{ stone_noun }}
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endcapture -%}
          <a class="btn btn--outline btn--block"
             href="{{ s.enquire_link | default: '/pages/contact-us' }}" data-fye-popup="enquire">{{ enquire_text | strip }}</a>

          {%- comment -%}
            "Set this in a ring" still waits on a destination: a guessed link on
            27,000 pages is worse than no button.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if s.setting_label != blank and s.setting_link != blank -%}
            <a class="btn btn--outline btn--block" href="{{ s.setting_link }}">{{ s.setting_label }}</a>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            The same wishlist snippet the ring pages use, so one saved list
            covers rings and stones alike. "Come back with my partner" is the
            normal behaviour on this page, and it had no way to.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- render 'fye-wishlist-button', product: p -%}
        </div>

        {%- if s.reassurance != blank -%}
          <p class="stone__reassure fine">{{ s.reassurance }}</p>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    </div>

    {%- if p.description != blank -%}
      <div class="stone__desc rte">{{ p.description }}</div>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</section>

{% stylesheet %}
.fye .stone__top {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  gap: var(--s10);
  align-items: start;
}

/* --- breadcrumb ----------------------------------------------------------
   Same treatment as main-product's. Both will move to core as `.crumbs` when
   a third consumer appears (conventions §2) — two is the threshold, and this
   is the second, so the NEXT one triggers the move. */

.fye .stone__crumbs ol {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: baseline;
  margin: 0 0 var(--s5);
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
  font-size: var(--fs-fine);
  color: var(--ink-soft);
}

.fye .stone__crumbs li { margin: 0; }

.fye .stone__crumbs li + li::before {
  content: '\203A';
  padding-inline: var(--s2);
  color: var(--ink-soft);
}

.fye .stone__crumbs a { color: var(--ink-soft); }
.fye .stone__crumbs a:hover { color: var(--sage); }
.fye .stone__crumbs [aria-current] { color: var(--ink); }

/* --- media --------------------------------------------------------------
   Sticky, as the ring page's gallery is: the table is long and the stone
   should stay in view while it is read. Bounded by .stone__top, so it stops
   at the foot of the block rather than riding over anything below. */

.fye .stone__gallery {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: var(--s2);
}

.fye .stone__stage {
  position: relative;
  background: var(--ivory);
}

.fye .stone__panel { display: none; }
.fye .stone__panel.is-on { display: block; }

.fye .stone__img,
.fye .stone__panel--empty {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 1;
  object-fit: cover;
}

/* The supplier's viewer is square, and its own page handles the interaction. */
.fye .stone__spin {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  aspect-ratio: 1;
  border: 0;
  background: var(--white);
}

.fye .stone__thumbs {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: var(--s2);
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.fye .stone__thumbs > li { margin: 0; }

.fye .stone__thumb {
  position: relative;
  display: block;
  width: 88px;
  padding: 0;
  border: 1px solid transparent;
  background: var(--ivory);
}

.fye .stone__thumb:hover { border-color: var(--sage); }
.fye .stone__thumb.is-on { border-color: var(--line-strong); }

.fye .stone__spinbadge {
  position: absolute;
  inset-block-end: var(--s1);
  inset-inline-end: var(--s1);
  padding: 2px 6px;
  background: var(--white);
  color: var(--teal);
  font-size: 10px;
  font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
  letter-spacing: 0.08em;
}

/* --- buy box ------------------------------------------------------------- */

.fye .stone__buy {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: var(--s4);
}

.fye .stone__eyebrow {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: var(--fs-eyebrow);
  font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
  letter-spacing: var(--tr-eyebrow);
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--ink-soft);
}

.fye .stone__title {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: var(--fs-h3);
}

/* The same words, in the same order, as the picker's stone cards. */
.fye .stone__identity {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: var(--fs-small);
  color: var(--ink-soft);
}

.fye .stone__price {
  margin: var(--s2) 0 0;
  font-size: var(--fs-h4);
  color: var(--ink);
}

.fye .stone__form { margin: 0; }

/* The buttons sit below the table as one group, so the gap between the last
   row and the first button is a single decision rather than two stacked
   margins. */
.fye .stone__actions {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: var(--s3);
  margin-top: var(--s5);
}

/* --- the characteristics table ------------------------------------------
   A specification, so it is laid out as one: fixed label column, hairline
   between rows, no zebra striping — the brand's structure is thin lines, and
   alternating fills would be the loudest thing on a quiet page. */

.fye .stone__spec {
  width: 100%;
  margin-top: var(--s3);
  border-collapse: collapse;
  border-top: var(--hairline);
  font-size: var(--fs-small);
  text-align: start;
}

.fye .stone__speccap {
  padding-block: var(--s4) var(--s3);
  font-size: var(--fs-eyebrow);
  font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
  letter-spacing: var(--tr-eyebrow);
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--ink-soft);
  text-align: start;
  caption-side: top;
}

.fye .stone__spec th,
.fye .stone__spec td {
  padding: var(--s3) 0;
  border-bottom: var(--hairline);
  vertical-align: baseline;
  text-align: start;
}

/* The label column is sized so the longest field name — "Fluorescence",
   "Mine origin" — does not wrap, which would break the row rhythm on a page
   whose whole job is legibility. */
.fye .stone__spec th {
  width: 44%;
  padding-inline-end: var(--s4);
  font-weight: var(--fw-light);
  color: var(--ink-soft);
}

.fye .stone__spec td {
  font-weight: var(--fw-medium);
  color: var(--ink);
}

.fye .stone__spec a {
  color: var(--teal);
  border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor;
}

.fye .stone__spec a:hover { color: var(--sage); }

.fye .stone__reassure { margin: var(--s2) 0 0; }

.fye .stone__desc {
  max-width: 68ch;
  margin-top: var(--s9);
  font-size: var(--fs-small);
}

/* --- breakpoints. Media queries LAST, always. ---------------------------- */

@media (min-width: 901px) {
  /* Sticky needs a top-aligned grid item: the default `stretch` makes the
     column as tall as the buy box, leaving nothing to slide within. */
  .fye .stone__gallery {
    position: sticky;
    top: var(--s5);
    max-width: calc(100vh - 180px);
  }
}

@media (max-width: 900px) {
  .fye .stone__top {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s7);
  }
}

@media (max-width: 560px) {
  /* The label column stops being a fixed share: at this width 44% leaves the
     value column too narrow for "Fancy Intense Brownish Yellow". */
  .fye .stone__spec th {
    width: 50%;
  }

  .fye .stone__crumbs ol {
    margin-bottom: var(--s4);
  }
}
